          "session_id": "full-mt-m-010",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I smoke and I have been diagnosed with COPD.",
          "answer": "A COPD diagnosis means you have a long-term lung condition in which the airways are narrowed and airflow is reduced. It usually develops over years, most often from smoking (*Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)*, General clinical reference). Smoking is the biggest factor you can change now.\n\n**Why stopping smoking matters most**\nThe respiratory guidance says smoking cessation is the only intervention shown to change the rate at which lung function declines (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis, group assessment and initial therapy*, Global respiratory strategy report). The heart-disease guidance in this corpus also says that combined behavioral and drug support for quitting works better than advice alone (*Coronary artery disease: secondary prevention*, cardiology consensus). My search did not return specific quit-smoking programs or treatment options, so please ask your clinician or pharmacist about what is available to you.\n\n**Other steps that affect how COPD progresses**\n- **Pulmonary rehabilitation:** A supervised exercise and education programme improves breathlessness and exercise capacity more than inhalers do. Being inactive because of breathlessness can make it worse over time (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis*; *Staying active with COPD*, lifestyle reference).\n- **Vaccinations:** Influenza, pneumococcal, COVID-19 and, where recommended, RSV vaccines reduce flare-ups (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis*).\n- **Inhaler technique and regular reviews:** Check that you use any inhaler correctly. Technique is often the reason treatment seems to fail (*Sticking with asthma treatment*, lifestyle reference). Reviews are recommended at least once a year and after any flare-up.\n- **Oxygen:** Long-term oxygen is used for people with low oxygen levels at rest. It is not a treatment for breathlessness alone (*COPD: spirometric diagnosis*).\n\n**Seek urgent care now** if you have severe breathlessness, chest pain, blue lips or fingers, confusion, or sudden worsening of breathing.\n\nI could not retrieve the details of the spirometry threshold or specific quit-smoking support. Your clinician can explain these, along with what your test results mean for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
          "risk_level": "routine",
